# NON-FUNCTIONAL STRAWMAN v2-d (imagination-tier; conventions per a-fire-morning's
# final human layer). The DIVERGENCE case: the world disagreed with the plan
# mid-apply and a guard fell through (proceed-and-flag, never a mid-apply
# question). Exercises: the SURPRISES aggregate section (leads when no trust was
# spent) -- apply-report vs plan-prediction (then-vs-now) -- honest in-book
# suspicion under the never-a-negative rule (positive pointer, ceiling named) --
# whylog timestamps as the narrative spine -- guard-doing-its-job framing --
# pure ASCII.

-- web.sh (context, abbreviated) --
 9  systemctl enable --now nginx
12  hork tune --profile web
13  ufw allow 443/tcp

-- world (context) --
No risk flag. At plan time (01:59) nginx reported enabled+active, so line 9
guarded (it sits below no wall at plan time -- but hork above it in book order
runs first at apply). At apply, hork ran at 02:00:12; line 9's guard re-asked at
02:00:41 and the answer had flipped: rc 1. The original bytes ran. Nothing is
broken this morning; the receipt simply has a surprise in it.

-- replay --
$ dorc why
receipt: apply 2026-07-25 02:00:37, host web1, trigger cron
   book web.sh (matches git HEAD 4c11f0a -- unchanged since the run)
   oracles: service 2.0, ufw 1.1, + base library
   risk-profile: none (no trust flags)
   plan: 2 ran, 2 guarded, 4 skipped (4 by proof)
   read top-down; every item is addressable: dorc why <line>

=== SURPRISES: the world disagreed with the plan ===

 web.sh:9 | systemctl enable --now nginx
   the plan reported "Service:nginx@{enabled,active}" at 01:59:52 and expected
   this guard to pass; at 02:00:41 the live check answered rc 1. the original
   bytes ran (2.1s, rc 0). the guard did its job: this is the fall-through
   working, not failing                                    (dorc why web.sh:9)

=== IMPROVEMENTS: dorc can do better, with your help ===

web.sh:12 | hork tune --profile web
   ran blind, as on every apply: hork is undescribed

~2 lines of sh: a convergence check for hork skips line 12 whenever hork is
converged                                                     (dorc teach walls)

$ dorc why web.sh:9
   [ web.sh, participating lines:
 9 | systemctl enable --now nginx
12 | hork tune --profile web

   === OUTCOME (2026-07-25 02:00:37) ===

   9|systemctl guarded, and the guard FELL THROUGH at apply: the fact that
   held at plan time did not hold when the line's turn came. the original
   bytes ran, exactly as written.

   === ANALYSIS ===

   what the plan knew, and what the apply found:

   * service.oracle.sh:12  reported  "Service:nginx@{enabled,active}"
                                     (ran 01:59:52, rc 0 -- plan time)
   * service.oracle.sh:12  reported  "NOT Service:nginx@{enabled,active}"
                                     (ran 02:00:41, rc 1 -- apply time, live)
   * dorc                  derives   something changed service state between
                                     those two reports. dorc cannot name it.
   ! 12|hork                         ran at 02:00:12, between them --
                                     undescribed, so unnameable either way;
                                     it is the only in-book actor in the window

   so: the guard existed for exactly this. a report is a moment, not a
   promise; the live re-check caught the drift and the line ran.

   === NEXT STEPS ===

   nothing failed; if the flip itself surprises you:

      suspect:  12|hork is the only in-book actor between the reports --
                but the window was 49s of real time on a live host; anything
                outside the book could also have acted. dorc can bound
                neither.
      observe:  `dorc plan --why web.sh web1` (re-measures now: is the state
                still flipping, or was this a one-time drift?)
      describe: an oracle for hork would let dorc say whether hork COULD have
                touched service state                      (dorc teach walls)

      review:   `dorc why web.sh:9 --all` (every link, unselected, exhaustive)

(Receipt analysis: the run's own records, nothing re-measured. Filtered for
